# Build context exclusions.
#
# Two things here are load-bearing and easy to break:
#
#   1. `.yarn/releases` MUST stay in the context. The image invokes the committed
#      Yarn 4 binary directly instead of bootstrapping corepack from the network,
#      so a blanket `.yarn` exclusion breaks the build. The Yarn subdirectories
#      are therefore listed individually rather than excluded with a wildcard
#      plus a negation, which is the pattern that tends to get "simplified" into
#      a bug later.
#   2. `node_modules` must be excluded, because the build stage installs its own
#      and a host tree carries darwin-arm64 prebuilds that would silently shadow
#      the linux ones.

# Host installs — the image installs its own (see note 2 above).
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Yarn state. Enumerated deliberately; see note 1 above.
.yarn/cache
.yarn/unplugged
.yarn/install-state.gz
.yarn/build-state.yml
.pnp.*

# Build output. Regenerated inside the image; a stale host `dist` copied in
# would be indistinguishable from a fresh build.
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and lint artifacts
coverage
.nyc_output
.eslintcache

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# Infrastructure and container definitions — not inputs to the app build.
# `infra/nginx` is the exception: its server config is COPYed into the web image
# (Dockerfile `web` stage), so the non-input subdirectories are enumerated rather
# than excluding `infra` wholesale — the same deliberate-listing rule as .yarn above.
infra/terraform
infra/scripts
infra/db-bootstrap
docker
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Docs
*.md
LICENSE*

# Local configuration and secrets. `.env.example` is committed but is
# documentation, not a build input.
.env
.env.*

# Editor and OS noise
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Logs and runtime junk
logs
*.log
*.pid
data
